 "verdicts": {
  "A CMU study (arXiv 2608.25202) notes that evidence on SDD comes largely from tool vendors, whose evaluations rarely extend beyond the authors' own projects.": 1,
  "Practitioners in the CMU study reported more reliable agent behavior from SDD but also greater planning and review costs, used it selectively and mainly for new features, and often included implementation details in specs.": 1,
  "The CMU study analyzed Reddit discussions, interviews with 21 practitioners (eight of them tool makers), and 266 repositories from SpecMine, a corpus of 470,795 spec files from 73,030 public GitHub repositories.": 1,
  "The CMU study found implementation detail in specs may help agents but makes specs prone to decay, and spec–code checks were described mainly when changes are completed rather than over time.": 1,
  "The CMU study suggests individual benefits may come from clarifying intent before delegation, while team benefits may depend on SDD becoming a shared process.": 1
 }
}